1
00:00:28,137 --> 00:00:29,980
The Channel on Modern Marvels

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,060
modern marvels it used to be that the only practical way to get from England's famed

3
00:00:42,061 --> 00:00:49,080
White Cliffs of Dover across the English Channel to the French coast was by boat the trip across

4
00:00:49,081 --> 00:00:57,000
the temperamental currents of the channel could be unpredictable and unpleasant now crossing the

5
00:00:57,001 --> 00:01:02,220
channel is a 35-minute snap a simple matter of shooting by train through the

6
00:01:02,221 --> 00:01:07,080
longest underwater tunnel in the world at speeds of up to a hundred miles an

7
00:01:07,081 --> 00:01:09,240
hour

8
00:01:09,241 --> 00:01:15,180
passenger trains can now speed from Paris to London in three hours

9
00:01:16,320 --> 00:01:21,560
shuttles carry trucks and cars which drive aboard remarkable open-ended

10
00:01:21,561 --> 00:01:24,800
wagons and line up bumper to bumper.

11
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:30,560
It's quite a pleasant experience, actually.

12
00:01:30,680 --> 00:01:35,080
It's much nicer than traveling on a boat or the sea cat or anything else because it's

13
00:01:35,081 --> 00:01:35,781
not so choppy.

14
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:38,100
The weather's guaranteed down under the tunnel.

15
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:39,940
So it's quite nice.

16
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:44,480
The Channel Tunnel, otherwise known as the Channel.

17
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:50,720
It's not much to look at, this long, narrow corridor in the ground, but make no mistake

18
00:01:50,721 --> 00:01:51,421
about it.

19
00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:55,620
The Channel Tunnel is one of the engineering marvels of our time.

20
00:01:55,621 --> 00:02:06,040
It took an army of some 7,000 workers seven years and almost $14 billion to drill this 31-mile link between England and France.

21
00:02:06,580 --> 00:02:16,760
They had to drill not one but three underwater tunnels in the harshest of environments, fighting bad rock, unexpected fissures and serious leaks.

22
00:02:16,761 --> 00:02:23,220
while tracking a rumpled path of bedrock that dips 150 feet beneath the channel floor.

23
00:02:25,620 --> 00:02:34,920
To drive a tunnel 30 miles through chalk and to then fit it out as a railway system,

24
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:39,680
to build two very large road rail interchanges at each end,

25
00:02:40,040 --> 00:02:44,660
and to complete the project was an achievement.

26
00:02:44,661 --> 00:02:49,760
It really is another wonder of the world that it was done at all.

27
00:02:50,260 --> 00:02:55,960
The chunneling team also had to overcome a cultural divide between England and France

28
00:02:55,961 --> 00:03:00,180
and a deep hostility to the very idea of linking the two lands.

29
00:03:01,080 --> 00:03:06,060
Here we were connecting two countries that hadn't been connected for 8,000 years.

30
00:03:06,061 --> 00:03:15,120
It was a huge advance in imagination, in technique, and in sheer persistence to get it done.

31
00:03:15,340 --> 00:03:18,380
The site workers used to say, you know, the biggest thing is the pyramids.

32
00:03:18,920 --> 00:03:22,760
I mean, as an engineering feat, it's absolutely phenomenal.

33
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:29,300
Before the channel opened in 1994, most people had to cross the English Channel,

34
00:03:29,660 --> 00:03:33,720
which averages 23 gale force storms a year by boat.

35
00:03:34,640 --> 00:03:39,920
The often stormy passage was one of the most heavily trafficked waterways in the world,

36
00:03:40,380 --> 00:03:45,760
with as many as 750 ships a day carrying people and freight between Britain and France.

37
00:03:46,860 --> 00:03:52,900
The dream of an alternative means of making the trip goes back at least as far as 1785,

38
00:03:53,340 --> 00:03:57,620
when a pioneering aviator flew over the channel in a hot air balloon.

39
00:03:57,621 --> 00:04:08,943
It was a Frenchman named Albert Mathieu, who in 1802 drew up the first serious plan for a channel tunnel, envisioning a horse and buggy passageway to be ventilated

40
00:04:08,944 --> 00:04:13,320
with a chimney-like shaft drawing air from above the waterline.

41
00:04:13,321 --> 00:04:18,500
The proposal was as bold as it was impractical.

42
00:04:18,501 --> 00:04:28,100
England and France were at war, and the idea of giving Napoleon direct access to the English mainland was not very popular, at least not among the British.

43
00:04:28,101 --> 00:04:33,760
Another Frenchman, Tommé de Gaumont, was the next champion of the channel tunnel idea.

44
00:04:34,580 --> 00:04:38,520
Des Gaumont proposed building an artificial island in the middle of the channel

45
00:04:38,521 --> 00:04:42,420
and linking it to each coast with prefabricated tube tunnels

46
00:04:42,421 --> 00:04:44,560
that would lay on the channel floor.

47
00:04:45,220 --> 00:04:49,420
To allay British fears, he included flood valves in his design

48
00:04:49,421 --> 00:04:52,580
that could inundate French troops in case of an attack.

49
00:04:54,340 --> 00:04:57,780
Other 19th century schemes for a cross-channel link

50
00:04:57,781 --> 00:05:00,920
included one for a kind of submarine on wheels

51
00:05:00,921 --> 00:05:08,260
and another for an underwater tube suspended by a long line of floating anchors.

52
00:05:09,340 --> 00:05:17,660
The invention of the tunnel boring machine, or TBM, in 1875 brought new life to the channel tunnel idea.

53
00:05:18,340 --> 00:05:25,060
This massive air-powered drill was, in its essentials, identical to the TBMs in use today,

54
00:05:25,061 --> 00:05:29,100
with a rotating drill head that could gnaw through the toughest geology

55
00:05:29,101 --> 00:05:32,500
at the then phenomenal speed of an inch a minute.

56
00:05:33,320 --> 00:05:38,660
In 1881, pioneering TBMs actually began drilling off the coast at Dover

57
00:05:38,661 --> 00:05:40,660
and on the French side at Calais.

58
00:05:41,640 --> 00:05:49,580
This 1881 tunnel was driven in the same chalk mile that we chose in 1985.

59
00:05:50,340 --> 00:05:53,220
The geologists knew that this was the best stratum.

60
00:05:53,221 --> 00:05:57,700
What they couldn't be sure of at that time was whether it went all the way across to France.

61
00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:04,260
I don't think they actually developed the notion of how you would get this tunnel all the way across.

62
00:06:04,700 --> 00:06:11,980
I think government said, prove to us that you can bore a tunnel in this material and that you know where to bore it.

63
00:06:12,420 --> 00:06:16,800
And that they did. And the tunnel is, what, about seven feet in diameter.

64
00:06:17,020 --> 00:06:19,360
You can walk down it, and we did walk down it.

65
00:06:19,361 --> 00:06:26,780
And this rotary machine cut a beautiful, smooth circular bore in the chalk.

66
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,620
So it demonstrated it could be done.

67
00:06:30,620 --> 00:06:35,560
After a year of drilling, each TBM had burrowed more than 5,000 feet.

68
00:06:36,220 --> 00:06:40,760
But as the tunnel became more of a reality, English invasion fears returned.

69
00:06:41,640 --> 00:06:44,880
And the government pulled the plug on the project because officials said

70
00:06:44,881 --> 00:06:48,500
it would fatally weaken Britain's national defense.

71
00:06:49,360 --> 00:06:56,640
Support for a channel tunnel persisted in the new century, and the British government finally

72
00:06:56,641 --> 00:07:03,040
commissioned borings of the channel floor in the 1950s to see if tunneling was geologically sound.

73
00:07:03,760 --> 00:07:10,000
As luck would have it, a layer of rock called chalk marl, ideal for tunneling because it was easy to

74
00:07:10,001 --> 00:07:16,548
cut, strong and impermeable, ran the breadth of the channel from Dover to Calais.

75
00:07:16,549 --> 00:07:17,680
Unfortunately,

76
00:07:17,681 --> 00:07:24,400
the chalk marl was sandwiched between unstable layers of rock and clay that could collapse if

77
00:07:24,401 --> 00:07:26,774
disturbed and trigger a flood.

78
00:07:26,775 --> 00:07:31,680
Worse still, the path of the chalk was narrow and furrowed and

79
00:07:31,681 --> 00:07:38,560
would be extremely difficult to follow. Nevertheless, engineers were confident that

80
00:07:38,561 --> 00:07:45,920
they could follow the chalk marl and in 1974 TBMs once again began to chew their way toward each

81
00:07:45,921 --> 00:07:51,800
other from either coast and once again the British backed out ostensibly

82
00:07:51,801 --> 00:07:56,420
because government funding fell through but also because the idea of connecting

83
00:07:56,421 --> 00:08:02,240
England to Europe was still highly controversial the Brits I suppose we're

84
00:08:02,241 --> 00:08:09,820
an island race and for generations we have felt safe from invasion because of

85
00:08:09,821 --> 00:08:14,400
this narrow strip of water and doesn't make any sense in today's terms but it

86
00:08:14,401 --> 00:08:18,700
stopped the Romans for a long while and it actually kept Hitler on the right side of the water.

87
00:08:20,020 --> 00:08:27,200
I can remember in the days when I was trying to sell this idea to the public in 1985,

88
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:31,880
having old ladies wave umbrellas at me from the front row saying,

89
00:08:31,881 --> 00:08:36,400
you rascal, you know, we don't want to be connected to the French. There's a danger.

90
00:08:37,640 --> 00:08:41,900
The idea of a channel tunnel seemed dead in the water, so to speak,

91
00:08:41,901 --> 00:08:47,580
until it was revived unexpectedly in the 1980s by the unlikeliest of partners,

92
00:08:48,320 --> 00:08:51,240
England's Conservative Prime Minister Margaret Thatcher

93
00:08:51,241 --> 00:08:54,540
and Socialist French President Francois Mitterrand.

94
00:08:55,680 --> 00:08:58,620
Neither wanted to risk government funds on the project,

95
00:08:58,780 --> 00:09:03,300
but both were willing to allow banks and other investors to put the bill and take the risk.

96
00:09:03,940 --> 00:09:07,820
It's really quite remarkable that these two particular leaders

97
00:09:07,821 --> 00:09:11,080
should have decided to get the act together and do it.

98
00:09:11,081 --> 00:09:18,320
But I think they each saw a wider political advantage to having their name upon this scheme.

99
00:09:18,920 --> 00:09:21,280
They knew that it could be done.

100
00:09:22,180 --> 00:09:26,220
A handful of attention-getting designs quickly came to the fore,

101
00:09:26,300 --> 00:09:31,160
including one for an outrageously long 21-mile suspension bridge,

102
00:09:31,720 --> 00:09:34,840
more than a dozen times the length of the Golden Gate.

103
00:09:34,841 --> 00:09:44,040
and this hybrid plan that combined two artificial islands, two tunnels and two bridges.

104
00:09:45,400 --> 00:09:52,220
The winning proposal for a rail-only link called Eurotunnel was reassuringly straightforward.

105
00:09:53,220 --> 00:09:59,560
It consisted of three tunnels, two of them 23 feet in diameter for trains coming to and from France,

106
00:09:59,561 --> 00:10:03,500
and a smaller service tunnel in the middle for maintenance and emergencies.

107
00:10:04,360 --> 00:10:10,100
The tunnels would stretch 31 miles from terminal to terminal, 23 miles of which would be underwater.

108
00:10:10,940 --> 00:10:18,060
The most daunting aspect of the proposal was the sheer amount of tunneling involved, 125 miles worth in all.

109
00:10:18,300 --> 00:10:25,420
In addition to the three main tunnels, large crossover chambers in which trains could change direction would have to be cleared.

110
00:10:25,421 --> 00:10:34,420
Cross passages linking the three tunnels every 1,200 feet and enabling people to escape in an emergency were also part of the plan.

111
00:10:34,421 --> 00:10:47,083
As were scores of piston relief ducts, smaller overhead connecting tunnels that had to be drilled every 800 feet to allow the pressurized air created by speeding

112
00:10:47,084 --> 00:10:48,420
trains to escape.

113
00:10:48,421 --> 00:10:59,030
The plan called for the tunnel to be built by a consortium of 10 major French and British engineering construction companies over a period of seven years at a cost of

114
00:10:59,031 --> 00:11:00,180
almost $7 billion.

115
00:11:02,740 --> 00:11:09,880
Over 200 banks would put up the money, making this the most expensive privately funded construction project of all time.

116
00:11:12,300 --> 00:11:14,980
The budget, of course, was more or less a guess.

117
00:11:14,981 --> 00:11:24,000
No one had ever built a tunnel like this before, and it would double almost to $14 billion by the time the tunnel was finished in 1994.

118
00:11:25,020 --> 00:11:36,415
A large chunk of that budget would be spent building a dozen 800-foot-long boring machines, mechanical molds, that could shoo their way through mile after mile of

119
00:11:36,416 --> 00:11:37,400
channel floor.

120
00:11:37,401 --> 00:11:45,740
The Babylonians built the first underwater tunnel under the Euphrates River in 2180 BC.

121
00:11:45,741 --> 00:11:52,200
The British built the second 4,000 years later under the Thames in 1843.

122
00:11:53,260 --> 00:11:56,740
The Channel will return on Modern Marvels.

123
00:11:58,120 --> 00:11:59,300
Watch a lot.

124
00:11:59,320 --> 00:12:02,080
Turn to the Channel on Modern Marvels.

125
00:12:02,081 --> 00:12:13,020
The first big question facing the French and the British as they prepared to drill three tunnels across the English Channel was the question of access.

126
00:12:13,880 --> 00:12:18,200
How to get people and supplies in and out of the underwater construction site.

127
00:12:18,520 --> 00:12:29,040
The British decided to drill two slanted passageways or adits descending from the base of Shakespeare Cliff, some 200 feet to the starting point of the tunnels.

128
00:12:29,041 --> 00:12:33,900
in addition they dug out three large shafts through which they could lower

129
00:12:33,901 --> 00:12:39,900
heavy equipment like the tunnel boring machines French on the other hand took

130
00:12:39,901 --> 00:12:46,020
a more dramatic approach digging out a spectacular trench called le Pouy de

131
00:12:46,021 --> 00:12:54,240
Sangotte the well of Sangotte 195 feet deep and 165 feet wide Sangotte was

132
00:12:54,241 --> 00:12:58,880
large enough to swallow the Arc de Triomphe of Paris.

133
00:12:58,881 --> 00:13:01,960
In a shaft of that size, all three tunnels

134
00:13:01,961 --> 00:13:04,120
could pass through the bottom of the shaft,

135
00:13:04,121 --> 00:13:07,360
which meant that you had vertical access to all three

136
00:13:07,361 --> 00:13:10,920
tunnels, and you could draw the material straight up out of them.

137
00:13:10,921 --> 00:13:13,180
And if any one of them got blocked,

138
00:13:13,181 --> 00:13:14,860
it didn't affect the others.

139
00:13:14,861 --> 00:13:17,780
The French TBMs were lowered piece by piece

140
00:13:17,781 --> 00:13:20,820
into the Songloth well and then assembled

141
00:13:20,821 --> 00:13:23,920
over a period of months in roomy starter tunnels

142
00:13:23,921 --> 00:13:27,300
that led to the smaller tunnels to be drilled by the TBMs.

143
00:13:28,120 --> 00:13:32,960
By early 1988, TBMs on both sides of the channel

144
00:13:32,961 --> 00:13:34,900
had begun drilling the service tunnel,

145
00:13:35,500 --> 00:13:39,460
the first of three passages that would make up the channel complex.

146
00:13:40,560 --> 00:13:44,240
Over the next few months, four more TBMs would set to work

147
00:13:44,241 --> 00:13:48,600
grinding away towards each other and excavating the two larger tunnels

148
00:13:48,601 --> 00:13:50,720
through which trains would eventually run.

149
00:13:50,721 --> 00:13:58,820
In terms of size, power and speed, tunnel boring machines had come a long way since their invention in 1875.

150
00:13:59,660 --> 00:14:07,000
These 1,400-ton behemoths boasted revolving cutter heads that stretched out as much as 28 feet in diameter,

151
00:14:07,560 --> 00:14:09,480
dwarfing the men who worked the machine.

152
00:14:10,740 --> 00:14:13,400
Rotating at three and a half revolutions per minute,

153
00:14:13,401 --> 00:14:21,340
the blade studded with clusters of steel teeth would grind through the hardest rock at 15 feet an hour.

154
00:14:22,400 --> 00:14:28,700
To actually stand at the face of one of these things is massively impressive.

155
00:14:29,100 --> 00:14:33,400
You're talking sort of seven meters across with all these little cups and you think,

156
00:14:33,540 --> 00:14:35,260
how on earth is this going to dig through the ground?

157
00:14:35,820 --> 00:14:39,440
But of course it does because it just literally eats it away as it goes.

158
00:14:40,300 --> 00:14:44,420
Interestingly, the British and the French did not use the same kind of TBM.

159
00:14:44,840 --> 00:14:47,700
The difference was not ideology, but geology.

160
00:14:48,900 --> 00:14:52,700
Six fault lines crisscrossed the first few miles of the French coastline,

161
00:14:53,100 --> 00:14:57,040
and that meant that any drilling would be inundated with water from the channel,

162
00:14:57,460 --> 00:15:02,320
pouring through the cracks in the earth, making work difficult and messy, if not impossible.

163
00:15:03,540 --> 00:15:08,300
During the aborted 1974 tunneling attempt, that's exactly what happened.

164
00:15:08,301 --> 00:15:12,720
And the French were determined to improve conditions this time around.

165
00:15:13,900 --> 00:15:18,720
It took 18 months to design and build a special waterproof TBM,

166
00:15:18,721 --> 00:15:25,440
a virtual tunneling submarine that could plow through the seabed in monsoon-like conditions,

167
00:15:25,740 --> 00:15:28,020
keeping equipment and crew bone dry,

168
00:15:28,580 --> 00:15:35,060
and that could withstand tremendous water pressure, up to 7,500 tons worth,

169
00:15:35,061 --> 00:15:41,420
The kind of pressure the TBM would encounter if water from the channel crashed full force into its path.

170
00:15:43,420 --> 00:15:50,140
The British, on the other hand, didn't expect a lot of wetness, so they drilled with more conventional open-face machines.

171
00:15:50,660 --> 00:15:58,740
It was a decision they would come to second guess, as unanticipated waterfalls made much of their drilling a very messy affair.

172
00:15:58,741 --> 00:16:05,280
the TBM of course was at the heart of the tunneling process eating its way through

173
00:16:05,281 --> 00:16:11,640
tons of rock while carefully following the skinny wrinkled path of chalk marl across

174
00:16:11,641 --> 00:16:16,620
the channel surveyors have fixed the difficult route boring thousands of

175
00:16:16,621 --> 00:16:20,880
samples from the channel floor and mapping the seabed with sophisticated

176
00:16:20,881 --> 00:16:27,740
sounding techniques a TBM driver sitting just behind the cutting head kept the

177
00:16:27,741 --> 00:16:34,220
machine on course with the help of satellite input and laser probes he has

178
00:16:34,221 --> 00:16:40,800
at his console a readout of precisely where his machine is with respect to

179
00:16:40,801 --> 00:16:46,100
where it ought to be and so he can steer it to keep it online it's if you like

180
00:16:46,101 --> 00:16:52,000
entirely blind flying because you can't see through solid rock but the machine

181
00:16:52,001 --> 00:16:57,440
computer tells him precisely where his machine is with respect to where it

182
00:16:57,441 --> 00:16:58,980
should be at any time.

183
00:16:58,981 --> 00:17:03,880
Behind the control room, the long body of the boring machine is busily

184
00:17:03,881 --> 00:17:06,024
engaged in two critical tasks.

185
00:17:06,025 --> 00:17:10,740
Getting the spoil out of the system and installing concrete lining

186
00:17:10,741 --> 00:17:16,007
rings to stabilize the freshly drilled hole. It's a massively impressive tool.

187
00:17:16,008 --> 00:17:17,020
The frightening

188
00:17:17,021 --> 00:17:21,840
environment, I remember distinctly the first time I went into a tunnel boring machine working.

189
00:17:21,841 --> 00:17:29,860
You had to go on to a very, very tight walkway with the conveyor going above you with all the spoil coming from the face.

190
00:17:30,360 --> 00:17:38,580
And you'd walk up through all this complex machinery that was lifting segments of concrete, these huge segment linings, nine tons each.

191
00:17:39,520 --> 00:17:46,824
And you had to literally creep through these tight, compact areas of the head of the tunnel boring machine to get into the little bit at the front end, which actually

192
00:17:46,825 --> 00:17:47,660
was doing the work.

193
00:17:47,661 --> 00:17:52,640
All the rest of it was mechanically driven, but right at the front, just behind the head,

194
00:17:53,060 --> 00:17:55,980
were the guys who were actually putting the linings up onto the wall of the tunnel.

195
00:17:56,560 --> 00:18:03,900
It would take about 20 minutes to erect a complete ring of lining behind the tunnel boring machine.

196
00:18:04,520 --> 00:18:11,320
So provided you could excavate that metre and a half ring width in the same 20 minutes,

197
00:18:11,480 --> 00:18:12,740
the machine never had to stop.

198
00:18:12,741 --> 00:18:17,780
One of the most impressive things about the TBMs was their sheer length.

199
00:18:18,500 --> 00:18:25,520
It took minutes to walk to the rear of these 800-foot-long machines, a distance of nearly three football fields.

200
00:18:26,060 --> 00:18:31,160
As many as 17 wagons loaded with support apparatus stretched behind the cutting head,

201
00:18:31,300 --> 00:18:40,200
containing everything from transformers to grouting mechanisms to ventilation and air compression systems to a lunchroom and an infirmary.

202
00:18:40,201 --> 00:18:46,700
The British alone produced and installed over half a million lining rings.

203
00:18:46,701 --> 00:18:53,860
The concrete used for the rings which support 150 feet of seabed and 150 feet of ocean had

204
00:18:53,861 --> 00:19:00,160
to be the strongest ever mixed, twice as strong as that used to line a nuclear reactor.

205
00:19:00,161 --> 00:19:04,440
All the granite for the concrete was quarried from a single mountain on the west coast of

206
00:19:04,441 --> 00:19:08,420
Scotland to ensure it was of uniform quality.

207
00:19:08,421 --> 00:19:12,560
assembly lines churned out hundreds of different types of segments including

208
00:19:12,561 --> 00:19:18,920
concrete rings that were 12 feet thick used to prop up weaker passages. Spoiled

209
00:19:18,921 --> 00:19:24,420
disposal was another huge and vital part of the tunneling process. Because their

210
00:19:24,421 --> 00:19:29,240
spoil was wet to begin with the French mixed it with water to create a kind of

211
00:19:29,241 --> 00:19:34,020
slurry which they pumped a mile outside of Saint-Gon and dumped atop an

212
00:19:34,021 --> 00:19:39,658
artificially created 125 acre basin called Fong Pignon.

213
00:19:39,659 --> 00:19:43,860
Six pumps, each one strong enough to dump

214
00:19:43,861 --> 00:19:50,660
enough spoil per hour to fill a baseball diamond to a depth of 36 feet spewed the slurry to the

215
00:19:50,661 --> 00:19:51,419
dump site.

216
00:19:51,420 --> 00:19:58,100
The British expected their spoil to be dry so they opted to haul it out by conveyor belt

217
00:19:58,101 --> 00:20:03,380
and train but once outside of the tunnel the Brits weren't quite sure how to dispose of it.

218
00:20:04,020 --> 00:20:09,640
various different options were looked at some of them requiring train loads of

219
00:20:09,641 --> 00:20:15,920
wet chalk to be taken around the country and lost somewhere there were various

220
00:20:15,921 --> 00:20:19,960
environmental concerns about where we were going to dump this spoil the

221
00:20:19,961 --> 00:20:25,120
logistics of moving it away quickly were very important and in the end it was

222
00:20:25,121 --> 00:20:29,400
decided that we would leave it as close to the hole where it came out of the

223
00:20:29,401 --> 00:20:36,000
grain as possible after looking at 90 possible dump sites authorities opted to toss the spoil

224
00:20:36,001 --> 00:20:42,840
into the channel adjacent to Shakespeare cliff the point at which it had been removed this proved

225
00:20:42,841 --> 00:20:48,720
to be an immense undertaking to keep the spoil intact so that it would solidify a huge seawall

226
00:20:48,721 --> 00:20:54,900
was built and driven into the sea the seawall frame was the largest sheet steel structure ever

227
00:20:54,901 --> 00:21:01,440
fabricated. As the five million tons of spoil rose up through the sea it

228
00:21:01,441 --> 00:21:08,140
congealed and eventually added 73 acres of landmass to England. While spoil teams

229
00:21:08,141 --> 00:21:13,580
tossed tons of rock into the channel, TBM teams desperately fought to keep the

230
00:21:13,581 --> 00:21:19,440
channel out of the tunnel. But leaks and the possibility of catastrophic flooding

231
00:21:19,441 --> 00:21:24,140
would soon become a major concern.

232
00:21:24,900 --> 00:21:30,740
Eleven men, nine British, two French, lost their lives building the channel.

233
00:21:32,040 --> 00:21:34,920
The channel will return on Modern Marbles.

234
00:21:37,880 --> 00:21:45,740
The biggest danger in drilling an underwater tunnel is the ever-present possibility of hitting a fissure and triggering a flood.

235
00:21:45,741 --> 00:21:56,640
This was especially worrisome in the channel because the tunneling team had to follow a thin, crooked path of chock marl that was surrounded by weak rock.

236
00:21:57,640 --> 00:22:02,660
Hitting bad ground could create cracks in the seabed and bring water pouring down.

237
00:22:03,860 --> 00:22:09,480
The thousands of boreholes that had been drilled to study the channel floor posed an even greater danger.

238
00:22:09,481 --> 00:22:15,480
Although you always specify that that borehole gets filled with concrete afterwards,

239
00:22:15,481 --> 00:22:19,480
contractors have a nasty habit of forgetting to put the cement in.

240
00:22:19,481 --> 00:22:21,480
And so you get a borehole full of stones.

241
00:22:21,481 --> 00:22:28,480
And you don't want in an underwater tunnel to intersect a hole full of stones that the water can rush down.

242
00:22:28,481 --> 00:22:37,480
So all we had to do before we started was to try to determine precisely where over 3,000 boreholes

243
00:22:37,481 --> 00:22:43,840
over 200 years had been sunk into the bed of the channel so as to avoid them with our boring

244
00:22:43,841 --> 00:22:44,541
machine.

245
00:22:44,542 --> 00:22:49,800
And because we didn't know with great precision where they were, instead of saying

246
00:22:49,801 --> 00:22:57,038
each hole is six inches in diameter, we said each hole is 30 feet in diameter.

247
00:22:57,039 --> 00:22:57,780
Miss it.

248
00:22:58,100 --> 00:23:03,460
The many borings, at least, gave engineers a pretty good idea of what they would find in the

249
00:23:03,461 --> 00:23:08,740
channel seabed or so they thought must have been one of the most surveyed bits of ground in the

250
00:23:08,741 --> 00:23:18,400
world and yet the tunnel boring machine on the UK side still hit unexpected wet ground just off the

251
00:23:18,401 --> 00:23:23,980
coast everyone expected it to be them to be going through dry chalk all the way and they had machines

252
00:23:23,981 --> 00:23:29,860
designed for that and so when they suddenly hit wet ground they were in a bit of trouble a bit of

253
00:23:29,861 --> 00:23:32,560
Trouble is a bit of an understatement.

254
00:23:33,120 --> 00:23:37,520
Water began seeping into the British end of the service tunnel in March 1988

255
00:23:37,521 --> 00:23:41,460
as the TBM gnawed its way forward just off the coast.

256
00:23:42,420 --> 00:23:45,160
The TBM was designed for a dry environment.

257
00:23:45,820 --> 00:23:48,700
But now, week after week and month after month,

258
00:23:49,100 --> 00:23:51,700
water was raining into the construction site.

259
00:23:52,360 --> 00:23:55,920
Salt water indicating that it came directly from the sea.

260
00:23:55,921 --> 00:24:02,360
I went into the face at the messiest point under the UK land drive.

261
00:24:03,440 --> 00:24:10,600
And if you can imagine a very, very heavy rainstorm that you're working in constantly at the face,

262
00:24:11,040 --> 00:24:14,060
that's what it's like, a very torrential rainstorm.

263
00:24:15,040 --> 00:24:17,040
All shift long, the whole time.

264
00:24:18,020 --> 00:24:25,500
The main concern was obviously that the ground was so soft that it would literally come in above you that it would collapse.

265
00:24:25,920 --> 00:24:31,240
and therefore the engineers had to be absolutely sure that they could proceed safely.

266
00:24:31,820 --> 00:24:34,760
So the choice was, do you try and turn offline?

267
00:24:35,960 --> 00:24:38,640
Do you carry on and hope the ground would improve?

268
00:24:39,220 --> 00:24:42,560
Or do you stop and find some other way of digging through it?

269
00:24:43,080 --> 00:24:46,640
And in fact, in the end, they made the decision to carry on slowly

270
00:24:46,641 --> 00:24:50,460
and as safely as possible in those conditions.

271
00:24:50,461 --> 00:24:55,800
But at that time, you were literally moving at a tenth of the speed that you would do normally.

272
00:24:55,920 --> 00:24:59,420
It was very, very, very slow.

273
00:24:59,421 --> 00:25:04,860
The leaks, as it turned out, were coming straight from the channel through very fine fissures

274
00:25:04,861 --> 00:25:07,760
that earlier tests had not picked up.

275
00:25:07,761 --> 00:25:13,360
The good news was the cracks were stable and while conditions were difficult and unpleasant,

276
00:25:13,361 --> 00:25:15,280
they were not life-threatening.

277
00:25:15,281 --> 00:25:19,600
Nevertheless, the constant leaks cost the crew a year.

278
00:25:19,601 --> 00:25:26,600
Later, in dry ground, the TBMs made up the time by drilling at the record pace of 1,200 feet a week.

279
00:25:32,600 --> 00:25:36,600
Drilling through the channel bed was the glamorous part of the tunneling process,

280
00:25:36,601 --> 00:25:42,600
but supply logistics was a major, if more mundane, challenge.

281
00:25:42,601 --> 00:25:49,980
A construction railway, the third busiest rail system in all of England, carried thousands

282
00:25:49,981 --> 00:25:56,060
of workers, hundreds of thousands of lining rings, and millions of tons of spoil in and

283
00:25:56,061 --> 00:26:00,280
out of the various passageways.

284
00:26:00,281 --> 00:26:07,960
The construction railway at its peak was 1,200 pieces of rolling stock, of which something

285
00:26:07,961 --> 00:26:14,640
like about 140 locomotives and the rest were supply vehicles and we were busier than Heathrow

286
00:26:14,641 --> 00:26:20,920
Airport in terms of movements and every single train went in fully loaded and came out bringing

287
00:26:20,921 --> 00:26:25,900
out goods and materials that had to be taken to the surface and disposed of all told the

288
00:26:25,901 --> 00:26:34,000
underground supply trains logged enough miles to go to the moon five times incredibly the tracking

289
00:26:34,001 --> 00:26:39,940
system used to monitor the incessant traffic was nothing more than a magnetic board covered

290
00:26:39,941 --> 00:26:41,317
with colored magnets.

291
00:26:41,318 --> 00:26:46,300
A more high-tech computerized system would not have been as reliable in this

292
00:26:46,301 --> 00:26:49,619
wet underworld. It was an unbelievable headache.

293
00:26:49,620 --> 00:26:52,800
You had all the different tunnels with all the

294
00:26:52,801 --> 00:26:54,012
different segments.

295
00:26:54,013 --> 00:26:58,220
Each segment had to be numbered and supplied exactly to the number

296
00:26:58,221 --> 00:26:59,320
that was going into the tunnel.

297
00:27:00,120 --> 00:27:02,560
You had what we called bullets of concrete,

298
00:27:03,240 --> 00:27:07,080
which had a lifespan of five or six hours maximum.

299
00:27:07,720 --> 00:27:08,660
So they had to go down.

300
00:27:08,740 --> 00:27:10,280
If they were delayed, they went solid,

301
00:27:10,880 --> 00:27:14,540
and then they had to be dug out of the bullets literally by hand.

302
00:27:15,380 --> 00:27:18,800
Interestingly, because of the congestion and long waits,

303
00:27:19,060 --> 00:27:21,320
and in order to keep down emissions in the tunnel,

304
00:27:21,840 --> 00:27:25,560
many of the workers rode to their job sites on mountain bikes.

305
00:27:25,561 --> 00:27:28,180
A lot of colleagues used to say that it was good fun

306
00:27:28,181 --> 00:27:31,000
going in on the bike, especially if you needed to keep fit.

307
00:27:31,620 --> 00:27:36,320
Because some of these guys were going up to 27K on a bicycle

308
00:27:36,321 --> 00:27:38,160
to the midpoint of the tunnel.

309
00:27:38,800 --> 00:27:41,460
The first 9 or 10K is downhill,

310
00:27:41,640 --> 00:27:43,380
and then it's very gentle from there onwards.

311
00:27:44,140 --> 00:27:46,840
It was only coming home was the difficult part, up the hill.

312
00:27:47,260 --> 00:27:48,160
That was a difficult one.

313
00:27:49,000 --> 00:27:53,280
Even more difficult, making sure that the two TBMs

314
00:27:53,281 --> 00:27:57,660
eating their way towards each other under 31 miles of channel floor

315
00:27:57,661 --> 00:27:59,540
would meet in the middle.

316
00:28:00,380 --> 00:28:03,380
After two and a half years of nonstop tunneling,

317
00:28:03,660 --> 00:28:06,560
the time had come for an underwater breakthrough.

318
00:28:08,120 --> 00:28:13,480
A British workforce of 3,587 lived in barracks at the job site,

319
00:28:13,481 --> 00:28:15,040
put in 10-hour shifts,

320
00:28:15,180 --> 00:28:18,720
and earned between $1,000 and $3,500 a week

321
00:28:18,721 --> 00:28:20,820
digging out the Channel Tunnel.

322
00:28:21,660 --> 00:28:24,340
Channel will return on Modern Marvels.

323
00:28:24,341 --> 00:28:28,740
Now return to the tunnel on Modern Models.

324
00:28:30,820 --> 00:28:36,920
One of the biggest questions hanging over the channel tunneling process was whether the tunnel boring machines,

325
00:28:37,160 --> 00:28:44,800
worming their way towards each other from each coast, over a period of years, would really meet in the middle of the channel as planned.

326
00:28:46,120 --> 00:28:52,440
The TBMs could be off target by as much as two feet and still have room to swerve towards each other and hook up.

327
00:28:52,441 --> 00:28:59,220
But anything more, a deviation of a yard over a distance of 31 miles would be ruinous.

328
00:29:00,380 --> 00:29:06,120
Tunnelers used lasers and geopositioning satellites to steer the TBMs.

329
00:29:06,680 --> 00:29:15,160
They also turned to micropaleontologists who analyzed tiny fossils in the soil to make sure they were on course.

330
00:29:15,161 --> 00:29:23,980
chalk was laid down millions of years ago in a big freshwater lake with thousands and thousands

331
00:29:23,981 --> 00:29:29,820
of tiny little shell creatures crushed by the weight of material on the top and forming chalk

332
00:29:29,821 --> 00:29:36,780
so in order to ensure that we knew exactly which piece of chalk we were in we had micro

333
00:29:36,781 --> 00:29:43,140
paleontologists employed who would take away a sample of the clay put it under the microscope

334
00:29:43,141 --> 00:29:47,020
and recognize these microscopic little shells.

335
00:29:47,580 --> 00:29:49,940
And if they didn't see the ones we expected,

336
00:29:50,060 --> 00:29:51,820
they would say, you're in the wrong stuff, chaps.

337
00:29:51,900 --> 00:29:54,100
You'd better alter the level of your tunnel

338
00:29:54,101 --> 00:29:56,140
until you find this correct stratum.

339
00:29:56,480 --> 00:29:59,900
So we were constantly drilling ahead of our machine

340
00:29:59,901 --> 00:30:01,080
and taking samples.

341
00:30:01,820 --> 00:30:03,700
On October 30, 1990,

342
00:30:04,180 --> 00:30:07,180
after two and a half years of continuous drilling,

343
00:30:07,181 --> 00:30:09,800
the French TBM had eaten its way

344
00:30:09,801 --> 00:30:13,680
through 41,612 meters of the service tunnel

345
00:30:13,681 --> 00:30:17,700
and was poised to break through on the British side.

346
00:30:18,580 --> 00:30:22,020
For the Eurotunnel team, this was the moment of truth.

347
00:30:22,580 --> 00:30:26,220
Over blasé engineers like myself may appear,

348
00:30:26,320 --> 00:30:28,420
of course it's a bit nerve-wracking

349
00:30:28,421 --> 00:30:30,380
because until you actually join,

350
00:30:30,980 --> 00:30:34,540
you can't be sure how precise you are.

351
00:30:34,541 --> 00:30:37,600
In order to finally align the tunnels,

352
00:30:37,601 --> 00:30:43,820
a small hand-excavated tunnel was driven from one tunnel to the other,

353
00:30:44,180 --> 00:30:48,060
through which we could, as we say, close the traverse of the survey

354
00:30:48,061 --> 00:30:52,660
and tell ourselves precisely what the junction error was.

355
00:30:53,480 --> 00:30:59,620
It was very interesting when you consider that we'd driven a tunnel 22 kilometers out from England,

356
00:31:00,360 --> 00:31:04,400
16 kilometers from France, and they had to meet in the middle,

357
00:31:04,401 --> 00:31:09,560
and the distance measurement between England and France was accurate to just

358
00:31:09,561 --> 00:31:16,260
about 75 millimeters three inches the alignment measurement was accurate to

359
00:31:16,261 --> 00:31:24,640
250 millimeters so we were pretty close that's through that's through

360
00:31:25,360 --> 00:31:28,640
that's the handshake we've all been waiting for

361
00:31:28,641 --> 00:31:36,260
Amid cheers, British TBM operator Graham Fagg walked through the opening and on to French soil.

362
00:31:36,940 --> 00:31:40,260
French tunneler Philippe Cosette crossed over into Britain.

363
00:31:40,640 --> 00:31:44,660
The two had their passports stacked and enjoyed the moment.

364
00:31:45,260 --> 00:31:51,100
Ladies and gentlemen, just about four hours ago, for the very first time since the end of the Ice Age,

365
00:31:51,101 --> 00:31:58,040
it became possible for the people of Britain to walk to France and for the people of France to walk to the United Kingdom.

366
00:31:58,640 --> 00:32:02,920
Differences between French and British culture, however, surfaced immediately.

367
00:32:03,960 --> 00:32:07,320
And when the tunnel junctioned, of course, there was great excitement.

368
00:32:07,680 --> 00:32:11,480
Nobody had walked from England to France for 8,000 years, probably.

369
00:32:12,180 --> 00:32:16,700
So when the hole was broken through, we rushed through into the French side,

370
00:32:16,780 --> 00:32:21,480
where there were two tables laden with geroboans of champagne,

371
00:32:21,880 --> 00:32:25,280
and a lot of them were already poured into flutes for us to drink, which we did.

372
00:32:25,281 --> 00:32:29,420
the French of course rushed into the English side where there were similar

373
00:32:29,421 --> 00:32:36,440
two tables laden with plastic cups and mineral water and the on top cordial

374
00:32:36,441 --> 00:32:50,441
nearly collapsed at that similar celebrations would quickly follow marking

375
00:32:50,820 --> 00:32:55,560
breakthroughs for the two other tunnels the larger passageways on either side

376
00:32:55,561 --> 00:33:01,140
through which trains would eventually run by 1991 the first and riskiest phase

377
00:33:01,141 --> 00:33:05,100
of the channel project was complete tunnels under the English Channel were

378
00:33:05,101 --> 00:33:12,880
at last a reality the stage was set for a new excavation challenge digging out

379
00:33:12,881 --> 00:33:17,580
enormous crossover chambers which had to be big enough to allow kilometer-long

380
00:33:17,581 --> 00:33:25,200
trains to cross over and change direction from one tunnel to the next the crossovers one on each

381
00:33:25,201 --> 00:33:31,500
end of the three central tunnels were the largest underwater chambers ever attempted they were

382
00:33:31,501 --> 00:33:39,900
excavated first one side then the other and then they they they ate away at the center section when

383
00:33:39,901 --> 00:33:45,720
you went in there during the construction phase it was awesome watching them bit by bit piece by

384
00:33:45,721 --> 00:33:53,640
piece section by section line the walls put the tracks in put the floor down put the doors in

385
00:33:53,641 --> 00:33:59,700
and all of it had to be taken in bit by bit and constructed in situ it's absolutely fantastic but

386
00:33:59,701 --> 00:34:04,620
they just did it like piecing a giant jigsaw together in this massive cavern the caverns

387
00:34:04,621 --> 00:34:10,320
were too large for the TBM teams to quickly install concrete lining rings as they had done in the

388
00:34:10,321 --> 00:34:16,260
tunnels. Instead they used a temporary support technique called shotcrete,

389
00:34:16,261 --> 00:34:20,840
spraying a layer of concrete onto the high ceilings and walls to keep them

390
00:34:20,841 --> 00:34:29,160
from crumbling. Shotcrete is a thin skin of sprayed concrete to spray directly

391
00:34:29,161 --> 00:34:35,220
onto the rock. It creates, if you like, an eggshell structure within the rock

392
00:34:35,221 --> 00:34:40,300
which will stand, as we know, for a considerable period and that gives us

393
00:34:40,301 --> 00:34:46,880
us time to advance through and take out all the excavation and then we can cast inside

394
00:34:46,881 --> 00:34:51,040
it a concrete lining at our leisure.

395
00:34:51,041 --> 00:34:55,580
The stability of the concrete walls would be severely tested soon after the channel

396
00:34:55,581 --> 00:35:01,960
opened in a devastating trial by fire.

397
00:35:01,961 --> 00:35:07,880
Electrified fences, baited traps, pits and dart guns all protect the channel from rabid

398
00:35:07,881 --> 00:35:17,700
foxes and terrorist attack. Modern Marvels will return. Now return to the

399
00:35:17,701 --> 00:35:24,140
tunnel on Modern Marvels. Putting a train tunnel through the English Channel

400
00:35:24,141 --> 00:35:29,720
involved more than, well, just putting a tunnel through the channel. 31 mile

401
00:35:29,721 --> 00:35:36,080
passageway, 23 miles of it underwater requires its own infrastructure including

402
00:35:36,081 --> 00:35:42,460
cooling, pumping and ventilation systems that make the whole operation efficient and safe.

403
00:35:42,461 --> 00:35:46,836
This is actually W1 pump station, one of three all told.

404
00:35:46,837 --> 00:35:51,680
You've got two on the English side and one on the French side.

405
00:35:51,681 --> 00:35:58,280
Five pumping stations like the service tunnel are responsible for keeping the

406
00:35:58,281 --> 00:36:03,520
underground pathways dry. Engineers plan for water to seep down from the

407
00:36:03,521 --> 00:36:07,360
channel at the rate of a hundred and fifty liters per second which sounds

408
00:36:07,361 --> 00:36:12,360
like a lot but works out to a liter per kilometer minimal seepage is a good

409
00:36:12,361 --> 00:36:16,700
thing relieving some of the stress created by the immense weight of the

410
00:36:16,701 --> 00:36:24,160
seabed and the channel pipes are everywhere in the channel complex pipes

411
00:36:24,161 --> 00:36:29,640
that keep it dry and pipes that keep it cool carrying cold water through all the

412
00:36:29,641 --> 00:36:34,200
passageways if we didn't call the tunnel the friction of the trains moving

413
00:36:34,201 --> 00:36:39,120
through the air would cause the temperature to rise to about 30 to 50

414
00:36:39,121 --> 00:36:43,200
degrees centigrade which is somewhere in the region of about 120 140 degrees

415
00:36:43,201 --> 00:36:48,880
Fahrenheit this would mean that air conditioning of the through trains and

416
00:36:48,881 --> 00:36:54,280
the shuttles would be extremely expensive in fuel and you'd get a

417
00:36:54,281 --> 00:36:59,460
circular argument of heat generated air conditioning throwing out more heat

418
00:36:59,461 --> 00:37:01,540
and therefore we have to cool it.

419
00:37:02,300 --> 00:37:06,500
The cold water keeps the temperature in the channel a constant 50 degrees.

420
00:37:07,280 --> 00:37:13,320
The water in the pipes is chilled here at a cooling plant at the foot of Shakespeare Cliff,

421
00:37:13,920 --> 00:37:16,860
next to the original entry point to the tunnel work site.

422
00:37:17,780 --> 00:37:23,840
When it's cold outside, a series of radiator-like fans are enough to keep the pipes cool.

423
00:37:23,841 --> 00:37:32,600
in warm weather enormous chillers are used four of them each one loaded with five tons of

424
00:37:32,601 --> 00:37:39,620
refrigerant the equivalent of 5,000 home freezers the chillers are among the most powerful in Europe

425
00:37:39,621 --> 00:37:45,740
each one can reduce the temperature of 200 gallons of water by six degrees in a single second

426
00:37:45,741 --> 00:37:52,200
As you can see, it's very windy, about 15 miles an hour.

427
00:37:52,201 --> 00:37:56,280
Ventilation of the channel is another vital issue.

428
00:37:56,281 --> 00:38:01,160
There has to be a way of getting fresh air into the passageways and of controlling smoke

429
00:38:01,161 --> 00:38:04,440
or toxic fumes in case of emergency.

430
00:38:04,441 --> 00:38:10,540
Huge ventilators housing gigantic fans, each one equipped with 34 blades to just that,

431
00:38:10,541 --> 00:38:17,160
blowing a constant draft, 161 cubic meters of fresh air per second through shafts like

432
00:38:17,161 --> 00:38:23,580
this one in the service tunnel, which distributes the air to the other passageways.

433
00:38:23,581 --> 00:38:28,640
Much of the air stays in the service tunnel, which is pressurized to provide a safe haven

434
00:38:28,641 --> 00:38:30,320
during a fire.

435
00:38:30,321 --> 00:38:35,300
By maintaining higher air pressure in here, smoke or other contaminated air can't seep

436
00:38:35,301 --> 00:38:38,320
in from the outside.

437
00:38:38,321 --> 00:38:44,000
The tunnel floors, of course, had to be paved and lined with track, over a hundred miles

438
00:38:44,001 --> 00:38:49,540
worth of state-of-the-art sensor-laden rail that doubles as a signaling system, shooting

439
00:38:49,541 --> 00:38:55,640
streams of digitized information to computers on board trains.

440
00:38:55,641 --> 00:38:59,320
Catenary lines were installed to deliver power to the trains.

441
00:38:59,321 --> 00:39:05,800
To normal use, the channel needs 160 megawatts of electricity, the same amount used by a

442
00:39:05,801 --> 00:39:07,598
city of 250,000.

443
00:39:07,599 --> 00:39:15,800
In May 1994, after centuries of talk and nearly a decade of construction,

444
00:39:15,801 --> 00:39:18,652
the Channel Tunnel was ready for traffic.

445
00:39:18,653 --> 00:39:22,060
A lavish banquet in the British crossover chamber

446
00:39:22,061 --> 00:39:30,579
marked the occasion. The Channel was built with three kinds of trains in mind.

447
00:39:30,580 --> 00:39:31,780
A passenger

448
00:39:31,781 --> 00:39:37,660
train that could speed from Paris to London in three hours, and two shuttles, a covered

449
00:39:37,661 --> 00:39:43,720
one for cars, and a larger lattice-framed one for heavy trucks. Both could cross the

450
00:39:43,721 --> 00:39:45,424
channel in 35 minutes.

451
00:39:45,425 --> 00:39:51,080
The shuttles were especially designed for the channel to make it easy for

452
00:39:51,081 --> 00:39:56,256
vehicles to get on, go through, and get off the trains.

453
00:39:56,257 --> 00:39:59,080
The wagons carrying trucks are

454
00:39:59,081 --> 00:40:05,760
the largest train cars ever built and boast the best speed to weight ratio of all time.

455
00:40:08,420 --> 00:40:16,840
These trains were of a completely new type because of the need to have a very quick transit

456
00:40:16,841 --> 00:40:24,000
through the channel and that led us to have trains running up to 160 kph and weighing up to

457
00:40:24,001 --> 00:40:24,836
2500 tons.

458
00:40:24,837 --> 00:40:31,440
Generally speaking in the railway world you have fast trains which are light or

459
00:40:31,441 --> 00:40:36,173
heavy trains which are slow and we have both here. That was a new fact.

460
00:40:36,174 --> 00:40:37,840
Critics had warned that a

461
00:40:37,841 --> 00:40:44,560
channel train could become the world's longest crematorium and fire was a major consideration

462
00:40:44,561 --> 00:40:45,788
in their design.

463
00:40:45,789 --> 00:40:52,080
The wagons are made from special alloys that can burn for 35 minutes the length of

464
00:40:52,081 --> 00:40:54,220
of a trip through the channel without warping.

465
00:40:55,340 --> 00:40:57,780
Unfortunately, the various fire detection

466
00:40:57,781 --> 00:41:00,280
and alarm systems were put to the test

467
00:41:00,281 --> 00:41:03,160
less than three years after the channel opened.

468
00:41:03,860 --> 00:41:07,880
On November 18, 1996, a truck shuttle caught fire

469
00:41:07,881 --> 00:41:09,860
as it entered the channel at Calais.

470
00:41:10,460 --> 00:41:12,840
Authorities believe that a torch was tossed

471
00:41:12,841 --> 00:41:14,940
